A native of Rwanda admitted that he set fire to the cathedral in Nantes
A runner from Rwanda, who worked in the Cathedral of St. Peter and Pavel in the French city of Nant on a voluntary basis, pleaded guilty to his arson a week after the fire.
About it reported on Sunday channel “France-3”.
According to him, the man, who was already delayed on July 19, the day after the fire, was detained again the day before.
On Saturday evening, he appeared before the investigating judge.
After accusations of premeditated damage, the man confessed that he organized an arson in three places - under a large body, under a small authority and an electrical shield.
Now he faces up to 10 years in prison and 150 thousand euros of a fine.
Motivation of the 39-year-old native of Rwanda has not yet communicated.
It only is known that he voluntarily helped in the cathedral and on the day of the fire had to close its doors.
However, according to confile recordings, he left a religious institution 15 minutes after receiving calls of a fire in the building.
The ignition in the Cathedral of St. Peter and Pavel took place on July 18.
More than 200 firefighters for several hours struggled for a monument of Gothic architecture.
Nevertheless, they did not be able to save stained-glaciers during the time of Ludovik XII and a unique Baroque organ.
To take the situation under the control only three hours after the start of the fire.
As a result of the incident, no one was injured.
The current fire is not the first during the existence of the temple.
In the year of 1972, the fire destroyed its roof.
To eliminate the afterfiliation, it took 13 years.
For parishioners the cathedral was opened again only in 1985.
