The actor of the Russian series responded to the accusations of “propaganda of LGBT and feminism”
The actor of the Russian series “Chiki”, whose creators were accused of “propagating LGBT and feminism”, responded to the claims to the film.
The authors "understood the severity of the topics raised and did not want to offend anyone", said the American Steven Thomas Oksner in an interview with the publication "Raising".
It seems to me that this is a story about the strength and general aspects of human life.
This has nothing to do with LGBT and feminism, ”said the actor, who played the father of the boy, who was dressed in women's clothes.
He stressed that he is “probated by work” and does not consider it propaganda.
At the same time at the same time, he added, he is freakized by the reaction of some people to the plot line of the series: for example, there was a case when "a person in a deep binge shot the set and said that all this is untided."
“There were a couple of cases when we met dissatisfied locals who didn’t know what we were doing.
But we were open, so the people who at first came with claims began to help us,” he summed up.
The case of the shooting, according to the publication, occurred in September 2019 in Kabardino-Balkaria: a drunken man attacked the car of the filming group with a weapon, after which he was detained by police officers.
Earlier it was reported that the public Commissioner for the protection of the family Olga Baranets sent a complaint to the interior Ministry and Roskomnadzor on the service more.tv, which placed the Russian series “Chiki”.
Baranets accused the creators of the series of “propaganda of non-traditional sexual relations and feminism”, drew attention to the fact that the main characters of the film work as prostitutes, and the series itself contains “stages of physical and psychological violence”.
The ombudsman was also outraged by the fact that “in the TV series pays a lot of attention to the homosexual sexual orientation” of a minor of the character.
The comedly drama "Chiki" tells about three prostitutes who began a new life and opened a business on the advice of a friend.
